00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.120
And I was very surprised just by like the screenshots of it.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.500
And yeah, it was what I thought it was.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:15.220
But yeah, before the podcast, never would have thought something like this would be on here.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:24.840
Right. And this is the game company Koei has a whole series of these like historical, not even Japan or live extreme beach volleyball.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.940
Exactly. No, they don't make dead or alive.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:29.980
Isn't it Koei Tecmo?

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:31.940
Didn't they merge? Oh, the merge.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.420
OK. Yeah. So technically, why wasn't Nobunaga in that game?

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.360
He should have been better off extreme beach volleyball.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.520
There you go. Little history for you there.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:47.920
But no, these games like they were huge on computers and Nobunaga is a line of games now.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.780
It's not even just Nobunaga's ambition.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.980
It carries on through the ages.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.680
But this is the first entry that comes to the NES.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.460
It's not even technically the first Nobunaga game.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:05.520
It's kind of interesting that like there was a game before this, I think, that came like Famicom or maybe Japanese computer only.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.540
But this is the first one for us.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:13.040
So it's also going to be our first foray into like this kind of strategy game.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:22.940
Sean, if you wouldn't mind, you're like you're kind of an expert in that 4X grand strategy category for, you know, for the podcast.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:28.240
I'm not saying you're like on the leaderboards of Europa Universus if there even is such a thing.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.900
But like, you know that stuff way better than I do.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:38.580
So just as an overview, right, like what is the appeal of these kinds of games?

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.020
Oh, wow.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.760
Yeah, the appeal of these kinds of games.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:04:05.060
I'd say that just the idea of having something that you can play while you're sitting down to have coffee and then stretching that into like taking up most of your afternoon and always having like problems to solve in interesting ways.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:13.420
And yeah, planning ahead and like system mastery that isn't like Twitch reflexes.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:22.120
I think like those are all the kinds of things that play into the people that enjoy strategy games.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:28.180
It doesn't matter if it's grand strategy or RTSs or 4X games.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:45.360
It may be maybe maybe less so RTS because the the slower nature of these games is really what makes it conducive to just like I just want to think I don't want to have to worry about how skilled I am at manipulating this controller.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:46.840
You know what I mean?

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.260
Yeah, that's a good point.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:55.940
Like a lot of people always mention the the permutations of chess and how like no chess game is ever the same and stuff like that.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:00.980
And even if that's like not necessarily true, it depends on how long the game goes on before it becomes a different game.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:05.360
I feel like these strategy games are really that like to the extreme, right?

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:12.540
Like even at a basic level of Nobunaga's ambition on the NES, you'll never play the same game twice.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:24.320
Like there's just too many numbers being crunched across too many different, you know, Dimos, the generals of the game, you know, there's just too much happening.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:31.300
And all the things are like random and the numbers that they generate are seemingly also random, but depend on the scores you have.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:44.220
This is a game where like no two playthroughs will ever be the same and everyone's going to experience, you know, a little bit of struggling, but also a very rewarding experience as a result if they if they can pull it off.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:06:04.480
Yeah, I think even with how little I could grasp some of the concepts like while I was playing, like there was those like aha moments that are kind of one of the defining features of this genre of like you're not you have no idea what you're doing in the beginning.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:13.720
And then like you learn one thing about the economy or like how to maximize your military potential and all those sorts of fun things.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:19.140
And even in it's even in NES game, like it still shares that DNA.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:24.700
And the game doesn't, you know, like it has a manual that does try to explain a lot of stuff.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.140
And nowadays we also have like the ability to go online.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:32.960
But I found like a really weird lack of information about this game for some reason.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:35.680
I don't know if it just like wasn't a popular seller or something.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:39.440
Don't get me wrong. I was able to figure out what each mechanic does and stuff like that.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:55.900
But I guess in some ways the it's ambitious of the game to just like throw you in to a main not a main menu screen, but the menu screen of your demo, your general, right, is like everything that's possible is just there for you.

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:58.720
And it doesn't say like, hey, you know, this is your first turn.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.840
You might want to raise taxes or give out some rights, right?

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:03.100
It's like, no, you can do whatever you want.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.080
You go to war on the first turn if you want.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:06.060
Like it's up to you, buddy.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:15.560
And there's something about just like that freedom and seeing all of your options available to you that made me want to like crack this game.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:21.680
Like it actually, even though I was doing so poorly, it made me keep trying to like, okay, I'm completely lost.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.280
I need to reset, start over and try something different.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:28.300
But I'm going to figure out how to make progress in this game.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.420
Yeah. And obviously, like you can go in so many different directions for it.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:45.440
But like the main objective of this game is to unite Japan or at least in the first scenario, unite central Japan like Nobunaga did in real life.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:08:00.740
Going back to the idea of there being not too much specific information in the manual, I think that kind of is like just a sort of a different way to teach someone how to play.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:12.880
Like nowadays for modern strategy games, you'll see like not really like a classical instruction manual because those barely exist anymore.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:24.220
But you'll get like patch notes that are like, oh, farming now gives you X times food times your skill, like whatever.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:27.880
It gives you like the real nitty gritty information.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:36.500
Whereas in a game like this, it just says like you need food to feed your peasants and also your troops.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:46.900
And like there's a lot of really intuitive ways that like maybe the game doesn't tell you that you need to have money to go to war.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:53.160
But once you try and go to war and you don't have any money, it kind of makes sense like at a base level.

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:59.300
You know what I mean? So I think like it didn't really need too much nitty gritty in the manual for this game.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:06.120
Yeah. In a way, having it be that, you know, like you just you don't have to worry about the numbers, right?

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:07.440
You're just figuring it out.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:15.080
And it's just kind of, you know, oh, I give the rice and these numbers went up, you know, all of a sudden like people are more loyal.

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:18.000
And the output is growing because people are hungry.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.120
People are no longer hungry.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:23.280
So they work harder. Like in a way, that's great, you know.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:40.560
And watching the numbers grow is probably the most satisfying thing that the game has to offer because that is like that's your screen for most of the game is you're just looking at, you know, a cute little avatar of your daimyo.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:47.540
And then all the different stats of your town or fief, I think they're called, right?

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.420
They're like, yeah, think of them like counties or states, if you will.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:53.860
That's your that's your area of the map.

00:09:54.740 --> 00:10:01.560
And and then otherwise, you're just kind of, you know, seeing what it's like, like a board game, right?

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:03.940
Everybody else's turns are just kind of simulated.

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:07.360
You're told what happens in terms of like big storyline things.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:12.420
If anybody had like a revolt or went to war with anybody, you are given the option to watch that stuff.

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:13.380
But you don't have to.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:15.580
I don't know why you would, to be honest.

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:17.060
It would just make the game go on forever.

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:20.220
But all that other stuff is just kind of happening.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:27.100
And so there is this, at least for me, there's this tunnel vision, though, of like, because it all happens on one screen.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:30.200
Maybe maybe it's game design.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:31.360
Maybe it's tunnel vision.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:37.900
But I feel like I get very locked into thinking that I'm making progress because numbers are growing and I think I'm doing the right things.

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:43.540
But then I find out, well, you know, I'm lacking in this other thing when I spy on a on another fief.

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:46.840
Right. Like all of a sudden I realize, like, oh, I haven't been training my warriors.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:48.820
But my town's really happy.

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:52.260
But now I'm going to go to war with somebody who's a lot better than me.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:55.780
And it's sort of kind of a combination between a couple of things.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:59.160
Like there's there's definitely like internal management.

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:05.440
Like you have to make sure that your your your people are happy, that your army is happy, that your army is trained, yada, yada.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.020
But you also have to, like, expand.

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:12.060
Like you're you're not supposed to stay in your one little county.

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:16.580
Like the name of the game is to become Shogun.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:25.420
So you need to keep an eye on what the kind of land that is being taken from the other daimyos.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:35.980
And because, yeah, they're kind of on two different screens and there's no real way to combine those with the screen space they have available.

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:44.340
It does sort of put you between two modes of playing and it like your mind is kind of split between them.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:50.180
And, yeah, I have also forgotten to focus on some things because it's just out of sight, out of mind.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:12:01.060
And even the idea that like even the fact that the menu is split into two pages, sometimes I'll forget about the other things I can do on the other page.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:11.280
Yeah, that's a great point, though, too, that, you know, ultimately the game's winning condition, right, is to conquer all 17 or if you choose the larger game, 50 fiefs.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:14.460
And the only way to do that is to continue to expand your empire.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:22.740
So you could spend, you know, the game is divided in turns and each turn is a season and then four seasons obviously make a year.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:29.660
And you could spend like a whole year growing your empire and being like, you know, oh, I'm doing really well.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:40.220
But then you find out like when you look at the map, like, no, everybody else is like kind of gained another territory or they've died or, you know, they're having a revolt or something like that.

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:50.160
So I found myself like when I first started learning this game, I was focusing a lot more on just like the building of my area and my town.

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:51.580
I think that's a good strategy.

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:55.660
Yeah, you know, like got to raise the taxes, but keep them happy.

00:12:55.660 --> 00:13:01.220
So give them rice and gold and then wait for the seasons to change so that you get that, you know, you reap those rewards.

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:19.940
Eventually, you're going to have to go to war.

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:32.100
And that creates like another game inside of here that is very similar to a turn-based strategy game where you're given a map and there's different terrains that are in squares.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.480
And you place, you know, your army in squares.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:43.140
And so does the opposing army that you're going to war with or if it's your own civilians, it's on your own map.

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:49.920
And then you have to win this turn-based strategy game of essentially, again, a different kind of set of numbers.

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:51.080
It's not pretty.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:53.420
There's no like graphics for your army or anything like that.

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:58.680
But you can see like I've got 20 guys in this square and they've got seven.

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:00.880
So hopefully when I fight them, I'm going to win.

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:02.600
Like it's all simulated in front of you.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:04.660
But it's a whole other game, right?

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:06.400
It is a whole other game.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:08.880
It's another like simple game on its own.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:25.260
But what makes it interesting is just how intertwined the management portion of like the empire management portion and the battle situations are.

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:33.980
Like if you're running low on food or if you were trying to play the rice market and you had just made a killing with gold.

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:35.860
But now you're low on food.

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:40.440
You're basically playing this and then you get attacked.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:55.060
You're basically playing like a timed mission of like I need to survive this siege and destroy this opponent on however many turns of rice I have left or it's game over.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:03.200
Like all those little interactions between like the training, like it's all building off of each other.

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:06.320
Yeah, that's a good point, too.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:12.000
But I also wonder, you know, maybe this is the time to introduce it.

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:15.140
But like when you go up against these computers, right?

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:21.800
And again, you find out about the stats like as a, you know, like spy kind of thing, right?

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:26.940
Like you have to have to have a spy like look at the other people to see what their scores are.

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:33.300
I don't know if it was just like my particular like losing strategy of building an army.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:40.220
But I found the computers to be very tough to handle when it came to the war aspect of the game.

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:46.020
It seems like their numbers are just, you know, always at least on par with what I'm going on, if not much stronger.

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:53.920
But also, you know, maybe the game favors a defensive strategy versus an offensive strategy when it comes to being at war.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:16:04.380
Yeah, what I think was weird about my situation is like all of my stacks were kind of equal in their makeup.

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:20.260
Like my command unit, which like is sort of an abstraction of like the daimyo's personal guard, would have like 20 people in it or 20,000.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:26.260
I don't know how they, I think it is that, I think it's like 1,000 per number.

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:27.440
Per number, yeah.

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:36.860
All of the stacks would have that and then I would look at my opponent and their big command unit has like 39.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:45.360
And I'm like, well, I don't know how to really, aside from just ganging up on him after killing all of his other guys,

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:52.700
I don't really know how I'm supposed to deal with that and how can I have my stacks have different amounts.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:17:06.760
It must be like kind of a thing that's just in a menu somewhere that I didn't come across because like the fact that all of the computers had this ability and I somehow didn't like makes me think that I'm the idiot here.

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:15.200
But things like that made me also think that the computers were playing with more knowledge than I knew.

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:20.480
I don't know what difficulty you were playing at, but yeah, I also had those.

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:22.940
I was told only play on difficulty number one.

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:24.380
I was the easiest difficulty.

00:17:24.580 --> 00:17:32.980
They said, a lot of people said that the game is hard enough as it is and you might as well just play on the easiest difficulty to get the game going.

00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:35.080
Yeah, I did that on one as well.

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:38.800
Were you able to change like how many people were in each unit?

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:40.620
So you can do that.

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:44.620
Oh. Yeah, it's in your menu screen, right?

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:49.680
You know how they have like 1 through 12 as like the options of things to do?

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:53.380
If you go to the right, there are more options.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:58.140
Yeah, yeah. Yeah, and the assign one lets you change.

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:06.160
Assign. Yeah, that lets you change how your loadouts are, how many number of troops you want, and how many are in each troop.

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:08.800
Okay, so then that was just definitely on me.

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:20.460
You know, that's a thing where even if you had the – I guess what I'm trying to get down to is, and this is something we haven't really explained yet, is when you choose your daimyo, right?

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:25.500
You actually do – each one of them has unique stats and has a unique location on the map.

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:29.400
You don't get put like in the dead center of the map each time.

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:30.760
You choose your location.

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:43.420
You're getting certain stats based off the daimyo you are, but then you also roll for a bunch of other categories too, like your drive and your IQ and your charisma and luck and stuff like that.

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:53.080
Yeah, that was surprising to me because that's the sort of stuff that I thought was going to be like the preordained stats that came with playing as that character.

00:18:54.480 --> 00:19:03.120
Were the stats that are inherent to each character kind of hidden because I thought that it was just those four or five randomized ones?

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:11.320
Right, so there are other stats like – like you probably didn't choose the same guy each time, right?

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:17.200
I was – I kept trying to go with Tokugawa.

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:27.800
Got it. Okay, so like if you would have gone with somebody else, it's not that those – there's like hidden stats or anything, but the way that you start off with your particular fief is –

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:29.700
Oh, like the town stats.

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:31.520
Yeah, the town stats are different.

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:46.960
And what's interesting about like that strategy of having like random roles assigned is like, yeah, it does give some like D&D-esque trait to the game where it's like it's also your player-made character and not just like this historical thing.

00:19:46.960 --> 00:20:02.260
But like if we're playing a game of history, I feel like, you know, if all these guys really existed, assign like meaningful IQ and charisma and luck stats to them based on things that happened to them in history, right?

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:04.400
Like that's the character you're picking.

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:12.500
It's almost like you're not rewarded for picking any particular character because you're going to roll and you could get terrible rolls, by the way.

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:14.220
Thankfully, they do let you re-roll.

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:22.560
But you could roll like anywhere like a score of 60 and mind 108 is like the highest I think you can roll.

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:31.240
But meanwhile, the computers are already like automatically generated to be in like the 120s, 130s.

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:33.400
Yeah, I saw that. I think that's some bullshit.

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:37.820
Maybe, again, it's like a balanced thing.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:50.520
I know just because going a little off topic now, even in modern games like AI is so hard to program that most grand strategy games aren't making smarter AI.

00:20:50.780 --> 00:20:53.920
They're just making the computers cheat more than you.

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:55.880
And that seems to be what's happening here.

00:20:56.680 --> 00:21:03.860
Right. So I think like at the end of the day, I'm not trying to say like the whole game is broken because of, you know, these randomly assigned stats.

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:06.200
But it is it is just a numbers game.

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:11.640
And I wonder like, you know, you already have so much to manage and deal with.

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:24.460
With the aspect of keeping tabs on what everybody else is doing, like even other people that you're not necessarily up against right now, because they might conquer like four areas and then you might conquer like two or three.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:29.660
And that's that is what it is, you know, and it's like you're going to have to go up with them eventually.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:37.400
And so I wonder, like, maybe you could give me some insight here, Sean, but like the balance of building your army versus your area.

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:39.500
Right. Or like your town.

00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:41.560
How do you deal with that?

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:48.040
Like, how did you personally deal with like, when is it time to go focus on your army and when is it time to focus on your fief?

00:21:51.340 --> 00:22:07.720
So in this game, I really wasn't paying too much attention to what was happening around me, or at least my decisions weren't being informed by that, because I felt so small already that just because like Takeda is starting to

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:12.620
blob all over the map doesn't mean that I can really do anything about it at this time.

00:22:12.620 --> 00:22:35.400
I was more trying to like taking an insular stance and trying to defend when I was attacked just so that I could get like my output up and my army not to rebel and try and make some money off the rice market so that I could expand or so I could build the town up.

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:49.860
So it was really like a lot of early game stuff that I think because I was kind of bad at this early game strategy that was sort of seeping into mid game where I should have been more boisterous and attacky.

00:22:52.240 --> 00:23:00.560
So it does give you enough information to strategize on both levels.

00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:14.440
But I guess I'm also used to having like a more robust notification system of what's happening in foreign affairs to work with that information better.

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:21.540
Right. Because at the end of the day, the game is about choosing, you know, what you don't manage, right?

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:25.800
Like as much as you have, like you only get one thing to do per turn.

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:34.880
So that choice is actually in some regards easier to choose what's the thing you're going to do versus all the things you're not going to do.

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:40.640
Like you have to look at your things and be like, oh, I'm struggling with the amount of rice I have compared to the amount of men I have.

00:23:40.780 --> 00:23:42.040
My morale is low.

00:23:42.200 --> 00:23:43.760
My dams could break at any minute.

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:46.040
So I'm going to have a typhoon on my hands.

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:54.660
But I right now really need to like assassinate the guy next to me so I can bid on his land when winter comes.

00:23:54.940 --> 00:24:02.720
Yeah. You know, I think that it's crazy that a situation like that can be described for this kind of game.

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:07.100
But I absolutely found myself in that position every single turn.

00:24:07.520 --> 00:24:23.720
Absolutely. And I think that that's the magic that I did not think that a game like this would be able to actually emulate just based on what I knew about the system going into this.

00:24:26.540 --> 00:24:39.740
But, yeah, I was so bad at this game that instead of like thinking about all of the intrigue and and war lording, I was just like, well, I don't have any money and the markets closed.

00:24:39.740 --> 00:24:41.180
So I can't borrow anything.

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:45.460
So I guess I'm just going to train because it's the only free thing that you can do.

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:54.480
Yeah, there's a lot of a lot of decisions to make and a lot of ways that you can get yourself into bad positions long term.

00:24:55.220 --> 00:25:01.300
Right. And don't forget that you have this whole like ticking time bomb that is, you know, like your age.

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:03.600
Yeah. And you're going to get older.

00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:05.800
Right. Four turns for a year.

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:06.820
You get older.

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:10.580
Your health gets lower as a result by one each year.

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:14.380
The older you become, the more likely you are to get sick.

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:16.500
And if you get sick, there's a chance you're going to die.

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:21.160
But also just like the less help you have, the more likely it is someone might assassinate you or something.

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:22.700
Yeah. So better.

00:25:22.900 --> 00:25:28.580
Yeah. Better get married to somebody and hope for the chance to have a son so that you can be the son.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:34.720
That's another. That's like, can you do you play as the son if you get married?

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:38.380
I never I would love I actually read something about that.

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:43.640
So I can't confirm if the NES version actually does it or if it's like a high end computer version of Nobunaga.

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.540
Nobunaga. I did it.

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:50.240
I said that last week and I thought that was funny, but I knew I was going to do it one more time.

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:53.960
But no, in this game, there is a marry option.

00:25:54.600 --> 00:25:58.160
And people are like, you know, basically saying good luck having a son.

00:25:58.580 --> 00:26:03.520
And that apparently if you did have a son, you would be able to go on and continue playing as him.

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:08.160
But I was not able to confirm that because I was never even lucky enough to get married.

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:17.260
Yeah, I was trying that out in the like as early as I guess like a half hour into the game.

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:18.760
And everybody was refusing me.

00:26:18.880 --> 00:26:24.500
And I think that actually like that that brings your luck down when that happens, which I think is funny.

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:30.520
You are now inherently more unlucky because no one wants to marry you.

00:26:31.300 --> 00:26:33.200
Yeah. Well, luck be a lady tonight.

00:26:35.660 --> 00:26:44.860
All right. So, you know, it's funny that there are the intricacies because I mean, that's a lot of those paradox games have like the whole concept of like just keeping your lineage alive.

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:46.740
Right. And like that's how you're survived.

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:50.840
This game has like that as like a fun little mini game almost like it's not necessary.

00:26:51.060 --> 00:26:52.980
But hey, get married and have a kid.

00:26:53.400 --> 00:27:01.800
Yeah. Why not? It felt kind of total worry to me because I think that you can do that even though it has a much more maybe even in this game.

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:03.700
It's a more like diplomatic strategy.

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:11.920
Sort of like how you can sort of bribe people not to attack you in this game.

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:13.480
Yeah. You can do so much.

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:15.200
You can bribe people not to attack you.

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:20.160
You can make packs with other areas and say like we're not going to go to war with each other.

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:37.000
You can hire ninjas to not just assassinate other daimyos but you can also do it so this way like, you know, I'm going to hire X amount of ninjas to storm into this town and, you know, talk about how much this place sucks so that the peasants like revolt against their leader.

00:27:37.180 --> 00:27:40.460
Like I think that's crazy that all of this is available to you.

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:43.480
And I don't know what the answer is.

00:27:43.560 --> 00:27:50.220
Like it would suck if there was like a mode in this game that like removed a lot of these things to make it more simple.

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:56.400
Right. Like so that's like, all right, you just have to deal with your gold and your men and your morale.

00:27:56.620 --> 00:27:58.760
You know, it's like that's not the game.

00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:09.400
No. Each individual mechanic in this game is so simple that if you remove one or two,

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:12.880
you've ruined the game as a whole.

00:28:13.360 --> 00:28:15.000
So that would not work.

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:23.700
Yeah. And I think actually almost all of the enjoyment of this game as somebody who, you know, has an interest in this kind of thing.

00:28:23.780 --> 00:28:27.440
I think all the enjoyment in this game just comes out of experiencing it.

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:33.680
Like you may, you know, like watching the consequences of your decisions and just seeing how far you can go.

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:38.620
Right. Like I don't know if I'm ever going to say that I beat this game.

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:40.080
I don't think that's ever going to happen.

00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:47.620
But every time I played it, I saw my defeats and I was like, yeah, like I totally deserve that.

00:28:47.820 --> 00:28:54.900
Like they had me outnumbered or that was a stupid decision to like focus on so much rice.

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:57.580
Like I died and I had like 190 rice.

00:28:57.840 --> 00:28:59.220
I shouldn't have had that much.

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:01.260
Like I should have been paying attention to the market.

00:29:02.140 --> 00:29:03.300
Oh, the market.

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:06.420
Yeah. You want to tell us about the market a little bit?

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:14.620
I really enjoyed that they had this as a as a another thing that you could do.

00:29:14.620 --> 00:29:24.320
And if you really wanted to, you're probably going to end up neglecting other other parts of other aspects of your empire.

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:31.600
But you could just like make money and and food by playing the rice market.

00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:45.360
And it's not that rice isn't the only thing with price fluctuations like the cost of hiring men goes up and the cost of buying arms goes up or down depending on demand.

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:52.000
And you can see your impact on the market when you when you make a purchase or make a sale.

00:29:53.800 --> 00:30:04.320
It's and like when there's a lot of a lot of states at war, you will see that the price of men of men just skyrockets to hire a soldier.

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:07.580
It's really, really interesting.

00:30:08.260 --> 00:30:15.160
Yeah, I actually can't confirm this, but I felt like men is the only thing that continues to go up.

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:16.160
In price.

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:17.760
Does it never come down?

00:30:18.360 --> 00:30:19.240
I'm not sure.

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:20.940
I think that was the case.

00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:26.060
And I thought that was like also just, you know, I like yours that like it could have to do with like wars going on.

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:29.460
Right. And like, you know, maybe that's just what makes them naturally more valuable.

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:39.520
But I was wondering, like, what was the game trying to say by having like, you know, like men be the only thing that like become more in demand as time as time goes on.

00:30:39.740 --> 00:30:41.960
But also just like the market in general.

00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:43.780
Right. Even if it is random.

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:46.480
Right. Like rice going up or going down in a particular direction.

00:30:46.900 --> 00:30:49.780
I think it's kind of crazy that you don't necessarily know.

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:57.380
Like, what if rice is tied to how much rice is out there across all of the fiefs?

00:30:57.580 --> 00:31:03.760
Right. And like if there's an excess of rice amongst all players, that rice in the market goes down.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:05.760
Like there's no way to confirm that.

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:11.780
But the mystery behind that is intriguing enough that I'm glad that like the manual doesn't answer that for you.

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:16.740
And there's enough like there's enough of an impact that you can make on the price.

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:26.040
Like it could just be coming back to like a standard range every after each like weird binary event of buying or selling rice.

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:31.380
Yes. But the fact that like when you sell a bunch of rice, the price of rice goes down.

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:36.240
It at least tricks you into thinking like, wow, I just participated in the market.

00:31:37.240 --> 00:31:38.880
Yeah. And you can see the effect.

00:31:39.480 --> 00:31:43.240
Yeah. The only thing this thing needed was the Nikeki 225, right?

00:31:43.940 --> 00:31:45.180
A couple of hundred years.

00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:47.280
A couple of hundred years early.

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:49.900
But I just want some rice futures to go, you know?

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:51.020
Yeah, exactly.

00:31:51.440 --> 00:31:52.960
Yeah. All right.

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:58.560
We're gushing over the game a little bit right now, but we're not really saying anything like we're not really saying we're doing anything in this game.

00:31:58.700 --> 00:32:00.460
Like, I don't know if we're even giving a review.

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:05.800
I think we're explaining how to play Nobunaga's ambition for people who probably have never played.

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:08.020
But it's hard.

00:32:08.020 --> 00:32:09.680
Is that the mission, though?

00:32:09.760 --> 00:32:11.960
I guess like what is the assignment here, Sean?

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:26.920
I guess like what I'm doing right now is I'm sort of talking about all the gadgets on this toy that we found because this is less like it's not like an adventure game.

00:32:27.040 --> 00:32:33.780
Like this is a machine with cogs and levers and stuff like that you play with.

00:32:33.780 --> 00:32:38.480
And, yeah, there is an objective of uniting Japan.

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:44.720
But there are just so many different ways to do that.

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:49.720
And I think we've been doing more than just describing it.

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:57.220
But, yeah, I don't know how to get a more analysis view of this game right now.

00:32:57.440 --> 00:33:06.760
Well, I think it comes down to the fact that ultimately, you know, even whether this game is a bad game or a good game, right?

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:11.900
It comes down to the fact that it is a game that must be experienced to understand what's going on, right?

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:16.680
Yeah. It's a video game in that regards in that I could play it.

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:18.020
The listener could play it.

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:20.200
You could play it. We could all play it even together.

00:33:20.200 --> 00:33:24.520
It does allow for up to eight players on the same game and stuff like that.

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:39.260
But we're all going to have like a different kind of takeaway on like because of the permutations and everything that's available to you on just what the strategy is, how fun the game is, whether you need all this information.

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:48.120
Like I don't know if we're the ones who can definitively say how to play this game, whether the game did it right or not.

00:33:48.280 --> 00:33:57.740
Like I would argue that the game itself is just simply giving you all of these options for you to decide what to do with it.

00:33:57.860 --> 00:34:04.180
The game doesn't say, you know, oh, make sure that you're doing this, this, and this to win.

00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:10.480
Like you can do – I imagine people who are really good at this game can totally say like, no, screw rice.

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:11.700
You actually don't need that.

00:34:11.880 --> 00:34:22.960
Like I figured out this thing where like if my men count is high and I just steamroll through people early, I'll have enough areas to control that like one can become my rice slave, right?

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:25.820
Like it's cornered off from the rest of the world.

00:34:25.920 --> 00:34:27.080
They grow all the rice.

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:34.340
At least I trade it to myself to send it to my other – sorry, the send option, option four.

00:34:35.280 --> 00:34:38.580
I send that to my other thieves and they just focus on continuing to build.

00:34:38.740 --> 00:34:41.620
And I think like that's the – maybe the aha moment.

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:44.860
But you might never have the aha moment in this game.

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:59.320
It takes a lot to even just – like think about how much time it took us through our playthroughs just to even understand what was going on with each particular menu input and seeing how that affects the game.

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:06.460
Because you can't just say, oh, I gave rice to my peasants so these things went up and that's all there is to it.

00:35:06.580 --> 00:35:11.220
Like those things – those stats going up then cause other things to happen.

00:35:11.360 --> 00:35:13.520
Yeah. So it's like everything is like this big domino.

00:35:13.520 --> 00:35:17.500
And the act of not doing something affects your game later as well.

00:35:18.280 --> 00:35:28.380
I think that this game is impressive in that in some ways it's giving you perfect information.

00:35:28.820 --> 00:35:37.140
So – and the things that you can do to affect the game affect it the same each time.

00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:41.400
So you have what you need to make decisions.

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:58.300
But the game is complex enough that there's some butterfly effect happening that is really what makes any strategy game worth playing is like the idea of metagaming to –

00:35:58.300 --> 00:36:10.220
sort of your example of having your food province and sort of funneling it to your capital, which I never got to the point of understanding this game well enough.

00:36:12.960 --> 00:36:23.300
And all those other little kind of taking advantage of the system that we had just sort of come to grips with.

00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:25.380
All that's there.

00:36:25.760 --> 00:36:30.160
It's just – it takes some time to get all of that.

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:33.140
Right. Just knowing what I know about the game, right?

00:36:33.220 --> 00:36:36.160
And I only ever played the 17 Thief version.

00:36:36.940 --> 00:36:38.160
Yeah. Too many variables.

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:42.680
Yes, exactly. Thinking about the 50 version, right?

00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:45.840
It's like that game – who knows what that's like?

00:36:45.980 --> 00:36:47.640
Like I can't even – I'm sorry.

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:54.040
But for the podcast, like there's just no way to understand – like it's just everything about this game is just scale.

00:36:54.140 --> 00:36:56.040
So I think we got the experience.

00:36:56.040 --> 00:37:01.300
But like this game can be as big and as in-depth as you need it to be.

00:37:01.360 --> 00:37:07.400
But you can also get like a satisfying experience from just trying the game out and taking it on.

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:26.160
One thing that I think I guess to get off of just pure description that I think I didn't like as much is that there's really only one –

00:37:26.740 --> 00:37:33.060
while you can go about doing this in the same way, there's really only one win state.

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:39.780
And that is basically if we're going to use 4X as a comparison, there's really just domination.

00:37:40.080 --> 00:37:49.740
You can only win this game by killing all of the other daimyos, which makes sense in that like that's historically what kind of happened.

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:55.920
There's no diplomatic victory.

00:37:55.920 --> 00:38:02.400
There's no cultural victory, even though there's no mechanics that really support that.

00:38:02.400 --> 00:38:19.640
Just like there aren't like different real play styles that you can do differently in this game as opposed to like a Europa Universalis or a Crusader Kings or a Civilization.

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:29.880
It's just like have enough rice and gold to support your soldiers and to be able to bribe people and you will win eventually.

00:38:30.260 --> 00:38:39.360
There's a lot of mechanics that are going in to support the production of rice and gold, but those are really the basics there.

00:38:40.240 --> 00:38:45.540
Yeah, you bring up a good point and I did have that as a note here of like let's just talk about the win state thing.

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:46.800
So I'm kind of glad you brought that up.

00:38:47.060 --> 00:38:51.420
The only thing I was thinking though is like since it's got the historical aspect to it, right?

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:58.760
Even if it was just, you know, you still have to conquer everything to become shogun and unite Japan, right?

00:38:58.980 --> 00:39:03.200
That doesn't necessarily have to happen via war, right?

00:39:03.340 --> 00:39:08.800
Like I feel like there could have been other interesting ways through town development.

00:39:08.800 --> 00:39:17.900
You know, like what if the peasants just really like you as a leader and now like loyalty on other fiefs is like changing to be lowered.

00:39:17.900 --> 00:39:23.860
And if it's under a certain amount, you can like just assume power through like a power dynamic shift.

00:39:23.860 --> 00:39:40.920
Exactly. Like if there was a stat or just a count of how much like respect or face that you have and there were different diplomatic actions that you could do that weren't marching in and burning a town down.

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:54.740
And if you had that much more prestige than your neighbors then and they were also weak, maybe they would just like swear fealty to you.

00:39:55.000 --> 00:39:58.320
And then you still had to nurture that relationship.

00:39:58.320 --> 00:40:01.940
Otherwise, they would probably support your enemies.

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:09.660
But that would be a stepping stone to also expanding your territory.

00:40:09.660 --> 00:40:16.380
And it's kind of just like a different way to do the same thing.

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:19.820
But at least it's a different route to take.

00:40:20.180 --> 00:40:21.380
I agree with you.

00:40:21.520 --> 00:40:28.500
I'm saying I think like that would have been an interesting new way to solve this whole thing.

00:40:28.580 --> 00:40:30.360
Like I don't know if they could add any more, right?

00:40:30.440 --> 00:40:31.520
Like who's to say?

00:40:31.640 --> 00:40:37.180
Like maybe they thought that because it happened that way in history, like that's the way it should go down or whatever, right?

00:40:37.180 --> 00:40:45.040
Maybe that defeats the whole purpose of them building this turn-based war game inside of the game, right?

00:40:45.140 --> 00:40:47.940
Like maybe that's why, you know, like they spend a lot of time probably developing that.

00:40:48.520 --> 00:40:49.760
Who can really say?

00:40:50.020 --> 00:40:57.780
But I think that's a very valid criticism to have of like the fact that it is only one win state just kind of pushes you in one direction.

00:40:57.960 --> 00:41:02.280
Like if anything, that is the one nudge that the game is forcing onto you, right?

00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:13.400
Yeah, but like that being said, you can go about your military brutal destruction of the countryside in any way that you wish.

00:41:13.520 --> 00:41:19.820
But you have to brutally force yourself on your other daimyos.

00:41:19.940 --> 00:41:21.320
You can't not do that.

00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:27.720
Yeah, and I wanted to go back to war for a second actually, the turn-based strategy part of it.

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:34.720
Because we were talking about that mostly from like a numbers and computer disadvantage thing.

00:41:34.860 --> 00:41:37.280
Like, you know, because mostly this game is about numbers.

00:41:37.460 --> 00:41:47.320
But they did an interesting like layout for these maps that just required like a different kind of strategy for a turn-based game.

00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:54.000
Like the way that the squares are laid out, they're on like diagonals to each of each other, right?

00:41:55.000 --> 00:41:57.580
So you can't ever just say like go to the left.

00:41:57.720 --> 00:42:00.100
You have to say up left or down left.

00:42:00.280 --> 00:42:07.080
Like there's no way to just like be on the same exact plane as another square unless it's up and down on the same square.

00:42:07.780 --> 00:42:11.480
Yeah. But then like because the terrains are also different, right?

00:42:12.080 --> 00:42:23.320
You have to like look at this map and really try to like think about how you're going to fight the opposing troops given that like you cannot pass over mountains.

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:30.060
And, you know, like for the water, like you're going to need boats for that kind of thing.

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:33.200
And like if you're in the sand and you're facing someone in the hills.

00:42:33.280 --> 00:42:35.100
You have boats? You're going to have the – what's that?

00:42:35.240 --> 00:42:45.240
Are there boats? There's not boats but you have like a water – I never had it but the opposing troops were able to come in from the water.

00:42:45.440 --> 00:42:46.780
Oh. And yeah.

00:42:46.960 --> 00:42:50.280
And they had like a little water icon next to –

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:51.460
On their tribe thing.

00:42:52.420 --> 00:42:53.320
I keep saying tribe.

00:42:53.320 --> 00:42:55.620
I think I mean troops. But you get the idea.

00:42:55.820 --> 00:42:59.560
Yeah. Anyway, like, you know, even just like the fact that terrains matter, right?

00:42:59.600 --> 00:43:02.700
Like if you're in the sand and they're on the hills, the hills have the advantage.

00:43:03.400 --> 00:43:09.600
And there is advantages to being in your village too as opposed to the other areas around you.

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:23.180
So all of this stuff makes for, again, like a lot of different choices but also for a different kind of war game where you are keeping your troops like almost entirely together in clusters, right?

00:43:23.300 --> 00:43:28.820
And just trying to like band together as one and take out the enemy before you get taken out.

00:43:29.460 --> 00:43:35.900
Yeah. There's room enough in the game to employ like different kinds of tactics.

00:43:37.260 --> 00:43:46.680
Like you could – you can be kind of spread out and hope that your enemy spreads out as well and sort of do like some hit and runs.

00:43:46.680 --> 00:43:50.320
Or you can, yeah, just have like a doom stack.

00:43:51.500 --> 00:43:56.920
But you're not really going to have too much agility in that.

00:43:56.920 --> 00:44:02.500
And you're only going to probably have like one front line to battle on.

00:44:03.780 --> 00:44:17.220
But I also think that the idea of only being able to move or attack is – it kind of reduces the amount of – the amount of things that you can do.

00:44:17.220 --> 00:44:28.460
Like it's definitely just as strategic of a way to play but there seems to be less variety of play styles.

00:44:29.840 --> 00:44:39.140
Like if you could have a move and an attack or two moves or two attacks, like I think that would also make this more fun.

00:44:40.020 --> 00:44:55.140
But I think that the war game, while yes, it is still very much influenced by what you're doing in the managerial game and that makes it more coherent piece of the game.

00:44:56.080 --> 00:45:03.320
But as a standalone, it wasn't like I was having all that much fun playing the war game itself.

00:45:03.320 --> 00:45:17.420
Yeah, and I think that's like again another thing that because the focus is on winning out these wars and conquering these fiefs, there's something interesting in a bad way, right?

00:45:17.540 --> 00:45:23.000
That like it's interesting that they've made this war combat thing and it's completely different from the rest of the game.

00:45:23.000 --> 00:45:30.600
But it's really basic and in some ways a chore or a slog to like get through.

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:41.360
You know, there's – it takes a while to whittle down the opposing troops and also to watch those fights unfold.

00:45:41.700 --> 00:45:50.260
And they're very like claustrophobic in a way because everybody is so tightly packed together and the mountains usually block off like –

00:45:50.260 --> 00:45:52.780
Yeah, they block you off from a large portion of the map.

00:45:53.600 --> 00:45:59.860
I think like they could never have made it desert – the desert commander, right?

00:45:59.860 --> 00:46:04.320
Like they couldn't have made it that game and have all those intricacies inside here as well.

00:46:06.520 --> 00:46:22.040
But maybe even the war side of it should have just been reduced to like numbers on a screen with you deciding like what kind of actions your troop will take and then being told the results rather than this layout of things.

00:46:22.220 --> 00:46:26.420
Like again, maybe this was supposed to be the exciting part of the game.

00:46:26.420 --> 00:46:27.760
I think it kind of fell flat.

00:46:28.260 --> 00:46:35.540
Yeah. I can't really imagine it just being a number cruncher behind the scenes.

00:46:35.560 --> 00:46:43.120
I feel like you do need some tactile interaction with the military part of this game.

00:46:43.120 --> 00:46:54.440
But maybe if I thought of this more as just like a management game instead of a military strategy game, I could accept that.

00:46:56.500 --> 00:46:58.880
Right, right. And I'm saying that off the top of my head.

00:46:58.940 --> 00:47:01.280
I have no idea of how they would actually accomplish that.

00:47:01.280 --> 00:47:07.980
I guess I'm saying like sometimes we've seen two games or three games in one, right?

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:09.900
And it works out for its benefit.

00:47:10.080 --> 00:47:20.280
In this one, I feel like in some ways seeing this whole new thing to do inside of a game that is already very involved is kind of like the hindrance.

00:47:20.280 --> 00:47:33.080
Like it is the do I want to keep doing this, whereas like the numbers menu screen thing flows pretty quickly and you just watch things happen, but you still feel like you're in control.

00:47:33.080 --> 00:47:51.040
I also think, though, that there were that it wasn't really like a zero something like you you could keep everything in the management of your of your daimyo or management of your town slash land the same.

00:47:51.260 --> 00:47:57.360
And yet just add a couple more wrinkles into the the the wargaming part of it.

00:47:57.480 --> 00:48:02.400
Like you have a unit that is riflemen or musketmen.

00:48:02.400 --> 00:48:03.820
And I don't quite remember.

00:48:04.420 --> 00:48:09.740
And I know that the range on these on those is like comically bad historically.

00:48:09.740 --> 00:48:30.700
But like just the idea of like your riflemen can attack from two blocks away and maybe your cavalry can move two blocks and just adding some variety to make it so that there are more interesting decisions to be made in this mode would have helped it.

00:48:30.700 --> 00:48:36.040
And made it less of a slog to play through because there are decisions to make.

00:48:37.420 --> 00:48:38.800
Right, right. Totally agree.

00:48:40.080 --> 00:48:56.700
I have one more thing I would like to talk about for Nobunaga's ambition, but I feel like before we can talk about that, we actually need to do the thing that we do on every episode, you know, where we vote on where the game stands, because I feel like we can't do the other part until we know where we stand.

00:48:56.960 --> 00:48:59.320
Does that make sense, Sean? What is what is this other part?

00:48:59.500 --> 00:49:00.560
I know it's OK.

00:49:00.560 --> 00:49:11.200
Right. So so guys, I'm just letting everybody know, you know, if you're that person who skips the essential games list because you think, you know, where we're where we're voting or where we stand on something.

00:49:11.400 --> 00:49:13.740
Don't do that this time because we're going to do something else afterwards.

00:49:14.040 --> 00:49:18.360
But first, we have to vote in a little thing we call the essential games list.

00:49:20.920 --> 00:49:22.960
Sean, I'm going to vote first, if you don't mind.

00:49:23.560 --> 00:49:25.320
Yeah, whatever. All right, cool.

00:49:25.320 --> 00:49:27.840
I mean, usually I vote last, so I thought it'd be exciting.

00:49:28.200 --> 00:49:30.900
Yeah, we're changing up so many things for this episode.

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:41.840
That's right. I don't want to go on like too long of a thing because I feel like the whole time we've just been reviewing the game in a way that like is is both fair to it and can be very generous.

00:49:41.860 --> 00:49:47.300
And then also like we have to remember that not everyone is really into this kind of thing.

00:49:47.440 --> 00:49:52.880
Right. This is not for people who like Super Mario Brothers, Kid Icarus, Legend of Zelda.

00:49:52.880 --> 00:49:56.760
Like, you know, you're not you're not coming in here and playing Nobunaga's Ambition.

00:49:57.280 --> 00:49:58.380
No, you could be both.

00:49:58.520 --> 00:50:00.320
Right. And we are clearly both.

00:50:00.500 --> 00:50:08.380
But I'm saying like for a lot of people, this is the kind of game that you rent at Blockbuster back then and you felt like you were tricked.

00:50:08.580 --> 00:50:13.500
You know, you thought you were going to get like you were going to play as some samurai in 16th century Japan.

00:50:13.500 --> 00:50:17.220
And instead you were greeted with, you know, a calculator game.

00:50:18.260 --> 00:50:28.560
It's not, you know, it's like clicker games in a way is a whole nother genre of the cookie clicker game where you just click on your mouse pad and you make cookies and you just let that stuff grow.

00:50:29.100 --> 00:50:29.980
Or paperclips.

00:50:30.420 --> 00:50:31.660
Some people love that shit.

00:50:31.660 --> 00:50:35.000
And other people are like, how could anybody waste their time with that?

00:50:36.100 --> 00:50:42.680
So I think like what Nobunaga's Ambition is doing here is really, really ambitious.

00:50:42.800 --> 00:50:45.680
And I know somebody was waiting for me to say that, but it's the truth.

00:50:45.860 --> 00:50:49.400
Right. Like what they did on the NES is just crazy to me.

00:50:49.540 --> 00:50:54.140
And the fact that like it also came out on a bunch of other things, including the Game Boy.

00:50:54.480 --> 00:50:58.340
Like it's crazy enough that this thing is on the NES, but it's also on the Game Boy.

00:50:58.660 --> 00:50:59.700
Like how?

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:01.180
How do they fit all this stuff?

00:51:02.600 --> 00:51:07.340
You could have a really good time playing this game or you could be completely confused playing this game.

00:51:08.460 --> 00:51:24.460
I just think that like because it's all done via numbers and because the numbers are being crunched in the background and it's not necessarily like a very exciting thing that's happening in the moment to moment of it.

00:51:24.460 --> 00:51:30.060
And you're not really sure of the advantages or disadvantages of what's going on in the world around you.

00:51:30.280 --> 00:51:34.100
I think it's really hard to be the Shogun in this game.

00:51:34.120 --> 00:51:42.080
And not on like a difficulty way, but on like a how much information they throw at you and how much information they also don't throw at you at the same time.

00:51:43.060 --> 00:51:51.720
I could see people being lost on this game and I think it's the little things in this game that make me think that it's not there yet.

00:51:51.860 --> 00:51:59.080
You know, it's a fun game to play, but it's a frustrating game a lot of the times too as you come close to the end of your playthroughs.

00:51:59.380 --> 00:52:02.740
You could sink in so much time only to lose.

00:52:02.740 --> 00:52:13.380
And I know that that's like natural in newer games, but newer games also spell a lot of stuff out for you and you're able to pull yourself back and see what's going on in the bigger picture.

00:52:13.560 --> 00:52:20.380
And also like you mentioned early on in the episode, Sean, like people know exactly what each thing is doing on like a number scale.

00:52:20.600 --> 00:52:26.400
Here we're just kind of watching it all unfold in front of us and that tells a great story, but it doesn't make for a great game.

00:52:26.520 --> 00:52:30.480
So this is not going on my essential games list, my vote for it.

00:52:30.980 --> 00:52:40.580
Sean, how about you? Yeah, I mean, so this is definitely one of those games that I like to think about more than I'd like to play.

00:52:41.760 --> 00:52:50.940
One word that I've used over and over on this podcast throughout the years and episodes is abstraction.

00:52:51.590 --> 00:53:00.380
And like between like strategy games and sports games to an extent, there's always a pretty –

00:53:00.380 --> 00:53:06.800
that's always coming into play of just like what it's showing you and what that's supposed to represent.

00:53:08.200 --> 00:53:13.940
And there's a sliding scale of how appropriate a level of abstraction is.

00:53:13.940 --> 00:53:19.640
And this is at a point where it's way too much.

00:53:20.660 --> 00:53:24.320
And that means that there really isn't much with presentation.

00:53:25.720 --> 00:53:32.900
A lot of the strategy games that I like to play are kind of like menu games like this.

00:53:32.900 --> 00:53:41.500
But there is still some presentational element that sort of reinforces that, oh, I made that progress.

00:53:41.500 --> 00:53:46.760
And, oh, that actually had a substantial effect on the game.

00:53:46.760 --> 00:53:57.000
Whereas this is just like, oh, your military now has a power level of X instead of X minus 35.

00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:03.780
So I also would say that it's really not there.

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:10.540
I'm still incredibly impressed by what they did here in terms of all the moving parts.

00:54:11.160 --> 00:54:15.720
But this is definitely a game that I like talking about more than I like playing.

00:54:15.920 --> 00:54:17.460
So also not essential.

00:54:17.700 --> 00:54:19.000
That's a great point, Sean, though.

00:54:19.000 --> 00:54:31.580
A lot of this game for me over the last two weeks that I dived into it were really searching for information online and trying to see how were people playing the game?

00:54:31.720 --> 00:54:33.240
What was the consensus on strategy?

00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:36.700
Learning about the systems in the game.

00:54:36.880 --> 00:54:38.280
And that was all so much fun.

00:54:38.480 --> 00:54:43.040
And then you take that information, you go into the game, and you try to use it.

00:54:43.140 --> 00:54:45.140
And you're like, nah, I still suck.

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:46.100
Something's missing.

00:54:46.240 --> 00:54:50.400
Something's wrong. Like, it's not going for me the way that they're talking about it.

00:54:50.660 --> 00:54:53.960
Or, surprisingly, no one's talking about it.

00:54:54.060 --> 00:55:02.320
Like, I was shocked how little information about this game is available online, you know, knowing that, like, it was ported to so many different things.

00:55:02.460 --> 00:55:10.460
I mean, this same exact game, Nobunaga's Ambition, eventually got ported to the Super Nintendo in the mid-90s.

00:55:10.600 --> 00:55:11.400
Like, imagine that.

00:55:11.400 --> 00:55:18.280
Like, the game that you could have bought in 1988 on 1989 on the NES is now on the Super Nintendo years later.

00:55:18.340 --> 00:55:19.200
And it's the same game.

00:55:19.200 --> 00:55:22.380
But now it has Super Nintendo mouse functionality.

00:55:23.480 --> 00:55:25.180
That would probably help, yeah.

00:55:25.680 --> 00:55:34.460
Yeah. I mean, that one also had a campaign mode where you can choose, like, different objectives and through different, like, years throughout the 16th century.

00:55:34.520 --> 00:55:35.440
So I thought that was interesting.

00:55:36.140 --> 00:55:38.260
But we're not here to talk about that particular game.

00:55:39.120 --> 00:55:40.440
This is my follow-up, Sean.

00:55:40.540 --> 00:55:42.560
This is why we had to do the Essential Games list first.

00:55:42.560 --> 00:55:48.140
Is that we're going to get Nobunaga's Ambition 2 on the NES.

00:55:48.460 --> 00:55:53.960
But we're also going to get other games from Koei that are in this same vein, right?

00:55:54.040 --> 00:55:58.480
Yeah, I see at the bottom here, Romance of the Three Kingdoms is coming.

00:55:59.000 --> 00:56:00.740
Yeah, Romance of the Three Kingdoms.

00:56:01.020 --> 00:56:01.940
Genghis Khan.

00:56:03.480 --> 00:56:06.540
I'm trying to think. I think there's at least one other one other than that.

00:56:06.540 --> 00:56:14.820
But a part of me is worried that we've done a lot of the heavy lifting here with Nobunaga's Ambition.

00:56:15.100 --> 00:56:26.320
And that games like Romance of the Three Kingdoms and Genghis Khan are going to be, you know, just different time periods of this foundation they've laid down here.

00:56:26.540 --> 00:56:33.340
What does Koei need to do for the other NES versions of these games to get on the Essential Games list?

00:56:34.160 --> 00:56:36.240
I think that...

00:56:37.080 --> 00:56:41.820
So in this episode, we've talked a lot about just, like, the genre.

00:56:42.100 --> 00:56:48.740
And we also put a lot of groundwork down for how we might talk about these games going forward.

00:56:49.840 --> 00:56:54.800
So we won't just have to talk about the most basic aspects of these games.

00:56:54.800 --> 00:57:04.460
But also, in terms of, like, how good these future games might be, I think that there's a lot that they can do to make these possibly essential games.

00:57:04.980 --> 00:57:14.280
Because, like, now that I've seen what they did this first time, I see all of these other possibilities that they could do going forward.

00:57:14.280 --> 00:57:24.140
And in these different time periods, the context also lends itself to new mechanics that they can implement.

00:57:24.140 --> 00:57:30.840
So I still am cautiously optimistic about how good these games may be.

00:57:31.660 --> 00:57:44.380
You're going to think I'm crazy, but I actually think the one thing that could, like, the one big thing that the future Koei historical simulation games could learn from is taking that menu screen, right?

00:57:44.480 --> 00:57:47.900
Where all of your information is, where all of your moves are available to you.

00:57:48.120 --> 00:57:49.480
You can still have that, right?

00:57:49.600 --> 00:57:51.540
You could have that be the groundwork for everything.

00:57:51.740 --> 00:57:54.340
But each, we need more submenus.

00:57:54.520 --> 00:57:56.440
We need more menuing in general.

00:57:56.440 --> 00:58:02.720
And I know that sounds crazy to say, but, like, take me, like, when I select tax my fief, right?

00:58:03.040 --> 00:58:07.840
Like, take me in there and show me, like, what are the current tax rates?

00:58:07.860 --> 00:58:10.540
Like, I know it does that, but, like, I'm saying, like, what are the current tax rates?

00:58:10.600 --> 00:58:13.340
Like, what's the vibe like?

00:58:13.360 --> 00:58:17.940
Like, what is going to happen to me if I make these decisions, you know?

00:58:17.940 --> 00:58:31.480
And, like, show that off in a way that's more meaningful where, like, each one of these 16 menu options has, like, a submenu that really, like, breaks down for you the effects of what's going on in the game to you.

00:58:31.880 --> 00:58:33.500
Yeah, I think that could be helpful.

00:58:34.660 --> 00:58:40.980
I also like the idea that it was just, like, show me who's being taxed and what will happen to their lives now.

00:58:41.380 --> 00:58:43.740
Right. But, uh, no, I agree.

00:58:43.920 --> 00:58:50.440
Like, more context makes for a better experience, I think, in games like this.

00:58:50.620 --> 00:58:57.860
And it was comically Spartan the way that they did it this time.

00:58:58.380 --> 00:59:00.820
Yeah. Yeah, I would, maybe they'll do that.

00:59:00.840 --> 00:59:07.240
Who knows? Yeah, again, like, even if, what if, like, so this is a Civ thing, right?

00:59:07.240 --> 00:59:14.640
But, like, where you get your gold told to you on your Fief, let's say I have 61 gold, right?

00:59:15.580 --> 00:59:18.500
That gold is generated, though.

00:59:18.620 --> 00:59:21.900
Like, I can continue to get more gold if I do certain things.

00:59:21.900 --> 00:59:30.200
So, what if there was, like, a plus seven next to it so that I knew I was gaining, like, seven gold a turn or something like that, right?

00:59:30.320 --> 00:59:40.640
Or, like, I knew how much rice I was going to be reaping in the fall rather than it just, like, being lumped onto me and I having to do some kind of math on paper.

00:59:40.840 --> 00:59:43.640
Like, I feel like I'm not asking them to automate things.

00:59:43.640 --> 00:59:45.560
I'm just asking them to outline things.

00:59:46.240 --> 00:59:52.320
Yeah. Obviously, those are, that's what modern games like this do.

00:59:53.440 --> 00:59:55.900
So, definitely borrowing from the future would be good.

00:59:56.060 --> 01:00:02.060
I guess the question is just how quickly they modernize because I don't know.

01:00:02.640 --> 01:00:12.820
Yeah. So, I think, like, that's a good place to, like, leave it off for now and we'll see, you know, if they're able to do our things or just surprise us in general with the other Koei games.

01:00:12.820 --> 01:00:20.900
But it will be interesting to see, you know, even just knowing that we're getting a sequel to this game on the NES.

01:00:21.120 --> 01:00:27.460
It will be interesting to see what that one does differently because this historical event only happened once.

01:00:27.860 --> 01:00:30.220
So, it's got to be the same thing, you know?

01:00:31.000 --> 01:00:32.220
Hopefully, they just made it better.

01:00:33.160 --> 01:00:34.560
I'd be interested in that take.

01:00:34.700 --> 01:00:36.440
So, we'll figure out what that is.

01:00:36.540 --> 01:00:38.140
But next week, a completely different game.

01:00:38.280 --> 01:00:40.940
We have to play Shooting Range, a zapper game.

01:00:42.160 --> 01:00:43.880
That sounds really basic to me.

01:00:43.880 --> 01:00:48.880
Mike, hold on. Was that your thing you had to do after this?

01:00:49.180 --> 01:00:51.420
Okay. Just making sure for the listeners at home.

01:00:51.700 --> 01:00:53.080
Checking the boxes. That was it.

